(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("secrets-pki",{"secrets-pki/the-secret-lifecycle-inventory-ownership-and-rotation":{"html":"\u003cp>Most secret incidents are not clever attacks. They are old passwords nobody remembers owning, copied into places nobody tracked. A lifecycle view turns a pile of strings into a managed set of assets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What counts as a secret and where they hide\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building an inventory with owners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ranking by blast radius\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rotation: scheduled, on demand and automatic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Revocation and the leak response plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-counts-as-a-secret\">What counts as a secret\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Passwords, API keys, tokens, private keys, signing keys, database credentials, webhook URLs with tokens and cloud access keys are all secrets. They hide in environment variables, CI settings, old chat messages, wiki pages, shell history, backup files and container image layers. Search for them as a routine task.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inventory-and-owners\">Inventory and owners\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each secret record what it protects, who owns it, where it is stored, who can read it and when it was last changed. An owner is a team that can decide to rotate it. A secret with no owner is a risk by definition, because in an incident nobody can tell if replacing it is safe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"blast-radius\">Blast radius\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Rank secrets by what an attacker could do with them. A read only token for one test service is low. A cloud administrator key or a signing key for releases is critical. Spend effort in that order, and aim to replace the highest ranked long lived secrets with short lived credentials.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rotation\">Rotation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scheduled rotation limits how long a stolen secret works. On demand rotation answers a leak. Automatic rotation, done by the secret store or the database, removes the human step. A design that supports two valid versions at once makes rotation safe, because clients can switch without a gap.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"revocation-and-response\">Revocation and response\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write the leak plan before it is needed: who is called, how the secret is revoked, how the new one is deployed, how to check the access logs for misuse and how to tell affected people. Practise it on a low risk secret so that the first real attempt is not the first attempt.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"inv&gt;rank;rank&gt;rot;rot&gt;rev\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The secret lifecycle: inventory, ownership and rotation\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"inv\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Inventory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rank\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Blast radius ranking\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rot\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rotation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Revoke and review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Inventory to Blast radius ranking; 2, Blast radius ranking to Rotation; 3, Rotation to Revoke and review.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"inv\">\u003cdt>Inventory \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Owner and purpose.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rank\">\u003cdt>Blast radius ranking \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Critical first.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Equal effort.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rot\">\u003cdt>Rotation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Two versions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Outage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Revoke and review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Logs checked.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgotten.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-key-that-nobody-dared-to-rotate\">Incident: A key that nobody dared to rotate\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cloud access key was found in a public repository and nobody knew which systems used it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam list-access-keys --user-name deploy-bot --query &quot;AccessKeyMetadata[].[AccessKeyId,CreateDate,Status]&quot; --output text\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam list-access-keys --user-name deploy-bot --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;AccessKeyMetadata[].[AccessKeyId,CreateDate,Status]&quot;\u003c/span> --output text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">AKIAEXAMPLE0001 2022-03-14T09:12:00Z Active\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The key was over four years old, had broad permissions and had no recorded owner. The team feared that disabling it would break production, so it stayed active for two days while they searched. They then disabled it, watched what failed and created a scoped replacement. Afterwards they built an inventory, set a maximum key age and moved the pipeline to short lived credentials.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No owner for a secret\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One shared credential for many systems\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rotation that needs downtime\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never testing the leak response\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping long lived admin keys for convenience\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-counts-as-a-secret","text":"What counts as a secret"},{"id":"inventory-and-owners","text":"Inventory and owners"},{"id":"blast-radius","text":"Blast radius"},{"id":"rotation","text":"Rotation"},{"id":"revocation-and-response","text":"Revocation and response"},{"id":"incident-a-key-that-nobody-dared-to-rotate","text":"Incident: A key that nobody dared to rotate"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","u":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html"},{"t":"AWS KMS concepts","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html"},{"t":"Kubernetes Secrets","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/"}],"text":"Most secret incidents are not clever attacks. They are old passwords nobody remembers owning, copied into places nobody tracked. A lifecycle view turns a pile of strings into a managed set of assets. What you will learn What counts as a secret and where they hide Building an inventory with owners Ranking by blast radius Rotation: scheduled, on demand and automatic Revocation and the leak response plan What counts as a secret Passwords, API keys, tokens, private keys, signing keys, database credentials, webhook URLs with tokens and cloud access keys are all secrets. They hide in environment variables, CI settings, old chat messages, wiki pages, shell history, backup files and container image layers. Search for them as a routine task. Inventory and owners For each secret record what it protects, who owns it, where it is stored, who can read it and when it was last changed. An owner is a team that can decide to rotate it. A secret with no owner is a risk by definition, because in an incident nobody can tell if replacing it is safe. Blast radius Rank secrets by what an attacker could do with them. A read only token for one test service is low. A cloud administrator key or a signing key for releases is critical. Spend effort in that order, and aim to replace the highest ranked long lived secrets with short lived credentials. Rotation Scheduled rotation limits how long a stolen secret works. On demand rotation answers a leak. Automatic rotation, done by the secret store or the database, removes the human step. A design that supports two valid versions at once makes rotation safe, because clients can switch without a gap. Revocation and response Write the leak plan before it is needed: who is called, how the secret is revoked, how the new one is deployed, how to check the access logs for misuse and how to tell affected people. Practise it on a low risk secret so that the first real attempt is not the first attempt. The secret lifecycle: inventory, ownership and rotation Flow in order: 1, Inventory to Blast radius ranking; 2, Blast radius ranking to Rotation; 3, Rotation to Revoke and review. Data store Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Inventory Data store What it does: Owner and purpose. If it fails or is weak: Missing. Blast radius ranking Step What it does: Critical first. If it fails or is weak: Equal effort. Rotation Step What it does: Two versions. If it fails or is weak: Outage. Revoke and review Security What it does: Logs checked. If it fails or is weak: Forgotten. Incident: A key that nobody dared to rotate Incident A cloud access key was found in a public repository and nobody knew which systems used it. $ aws iam list-access-keys --user-name deploy-bot --query \"AccessKeyMetadata[].[AccessKeyId,CreateDate,Status]\" --output text AKIAEXAMPLE0001 2022-03-14T09:12:00Z Active (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The key was over four years old, had broad permissions and had no recorded owner. The team feared that disabling it would break production, so it stayed active for two days while they searched. They then disabled it, watched what failed and created a scoped replacement. Afterwards they built an inventory, set a maximum key age and moved the pipeline to short lived credentials. Common mistakes No owner for a secret One shared credential for many systems Rotation that needs downtime Never testing the leak response Keeping long lived admin keys for convenience"},"secrets-pki/finding-and-stopping-leaks-in-code-ci-and-images":{"html":"\u003cp>A secret committed once stays in history for ever, and bots scan public repositories within minutes. Prevention, detection and a calm response are all needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Where leaks happen in a delivery pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pre commit and server side scanning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scanning history, not only the latest files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build logs, artifacts and image layers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Responding: revoke first, clean history second\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-leaks-happen\">Where leaks happen\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Typical leaks are a key in a configuration file, a token printed in a build log, a credentials file copied into an image layer, a secret pasted into a ticket and an environment dump in a crash report. Each pipeline stage is a possible place, so scan more than the source tree.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scanners-and-hooks\">Scanners and hooks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A pre commit hook catches many mistakes before they leave a laptop, but hooks can be skipped. Add a server side scan on every push and pull request that blocks the merge, and use the platform secret scanning and push protection features where they exist. Tune rules to cut false positives, otherwise people learn to ignore them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"history\">History\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scanning only the current files misses secrets removed in later commits. Run a one time scan over the full history of important repositories and over forks and archives. Remember that removing a file in a new commit does not remove it from history.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"logs-and-images\">Logs and images\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Mask secrets in CI output and never echo them. Scan build logs and artifacts. For container images, build with secret mounts, and not with arguments or copied files, because every layer is kept. Scan images for files such as private keys and credentials before publishing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"response\">Response\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Assume any exposed secret is compromised. Revoke or rotate it first, then check the logs for use, then clean up history if it matters. Rewriting history does not help once copies exist elsewhere. Record what happened and add a control so the same path is closed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"dev&gt;hook;hook&gt;hist;hist&gt;resp\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Finding and stopping leaks in code, CI and images\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dev\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Developer or job\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"hook\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Hook and scan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"hist\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">History scan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"resp\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Revoke and review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Developer or job to Hook and scan; 2, Hook and scan to History scan; 3, History scan to Revoke and review.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dev\">\u003cdt>Developer or job \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Writes code.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Pastes key.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"hook\">\u003cdt>Hook and scan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Blocks push.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"hist\">\u003cdt>History scan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Finds old keys.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never run.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"resp\">\u003cdt>Revoke and review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> First action.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Late.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-token-in-a-build-log\">Incident: A token in a build log\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A token for the package registry appeared in a public build log after a debug flag was turned on.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -n &quot;npm_&quot; build-4821.log | head -2\necho &quot;token is exposed in a public log&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;npm_&quot;\u003c/span> build-4821.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">214: //registry.example.com/:_authToken=npm_AbCdEf123456\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;token is exposed in a public log&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">token is exposed in a public log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A developer had enabled verbose output while debugging, and the tool printed its configuration, including the token. The team revoked the token within minutes, checked the registry audit log for downloads and publishes, deleted the log and issued a scoped token stored as a masked variable. They added a log scanner to the pipeline and turned off verbose mode for protected branches.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Relying only on a pre commit hook\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scanning the latest files but not history\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Passing secrets as build arguments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cleaning history before revoking the secret\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring false positives until the tool is disabled\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-leaks-happen","text":"Where leaks happen"},{"id":"scanners-and-hooks","text":"Scanners and hooks"},{"id":"history","text":"History"},{"id":"logs-and-images","text":"Logs and images"},{"id":"response","text":"Response"},{"id":"incident-a-token-in-a-build-log","text":"Incident: A token in a build log"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GitHub secret scanning","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/secret-scanning/introduction/about-secret-scanning"},{"t":"Docker build secrets","u":"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/secrets/"},{"t":"OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","u":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html"}],"text":"A secret committed once stays in history for ever, and bots scan public repositories within minutes. Prevention, detection and a calm response are all needed. What you will learn Where leaks happen in a delivery pipeline Pre commit and server side scanning Scanning history, not only the latest files Build logs, artifacts and image layers Responding: revoke first, clean history second Where leaks happen Typical leaks are a key in a configuration file, a token printed in a build log, a credentials file copied into an image layer, a secret pasted into a ticket and an environment dump in a crash report. Each pipeline stage is a possible place, so scan more than the source tree. Scanners and hooks A pre commit hook catches many mistakes before they leave a laptop, but hooks can be skipped. Add a server side scan on every push and pull request that blocks the merge, and use the platform secret scanning and push protection features where they exist. Tune rules to cut false positives, otherwise people learn to ignore them. History Scanning only the current files misses secrets removed in later commits. Run a one time scan over the full history of important repositories and over forks and archives. Remember that removing a file in a new commit does not remove it from history. Logs and images Mask secrets in CI output and never echo them. Scan build logs and artifacts. For container images, build with secret mounts, and not with arguments or copied files, because every layer is kept. Scan images for files such as private keys and credentials before publishing. Response Assume any exposed secret is compromised. Revoke or rotate it first, then check the logs for use, then clean up history if it matters. Rewriting history does not help once copies exist elsewhere. Record what happened and add a control so the same path is closed. Finding and stopping leaks in code, CI and images Flow in order: 1, Developer or job to Hook and scan; 2, Hook and scan to History scan; 3, History scan to Revoke and review. Outside party Security Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Developer or job Outside party What it does: Writes code. If it fails or is weak: Pastes key. Hook and scan Security What it does: Blocks push. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. History scan Observability What it does: Finds old keys. If it fails or is weak: Never run. Revoke and review Step What it does: First action. If it fails or is weak: Late. Incident: A token in a build log Incident A token for the package registry appeared in a public build log after a debug flag was turned on. $ grep -n \"npm_\" build-4821.log | head -2 214: //registry.example.com/:_authToken=npm_AbCdEf123456 $ echo \"token is exposed in a public log\" token is exposed in a public log (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A developer had enabled verbose output while debugging, and the tool printed its configuration, including the token. The team revoked the token within minutes, checked the registry audit log for downloads and publishes, deleted the log and issued a scoped token stored as a masked variable. They added a log scanner to the pipeline and turned off verbose mode for protected branches. Common mistakes Relying only on a pre commit hook Scanning the latest files but not history Passing secrets as build arguments Cleaning history before revoking the secret Ignoring false positives until the tool is disabled"},"secrets-pki/delivering-secrets-to-kubernetes-workloads":{"html":"\u003cp>A Kubernetes Secret is encoded, not encrypted by default, and anyone with read access to it or to etcd can see it. The question is where the real secret lives and how it reaches the pod.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a native Secret does and does not protect\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>External Secrets Operator: sync from a store\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets Store CSI driver: mount at start\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Encrypted files in a repository with SOPS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Environment variables versus files and refresh\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-secrets\">Native Secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Secret is base64 encoded data stored in etcd. Enable encryption at rest for etcd, restrict who can get and list Secrets with RBAC, and avoid mounting them where not needed. Treat access to a namespace Secret as access to the secret, because pods in the namespace can read it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"external-secrets-operator\">External Secrets Operator\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The operator reads values from an external store such as a cloud secret manager or Vault and creates native Secrets in the cluster. You describe the mapping in a custom resource, and the operator refreshes it on an interval. This keeps the source of truth outside the cluster and supports rotation, at the cost that the values still exist as Secrets inside.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets-store-csi-driver\">Secrets Store CSI driver\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The driver mounts secrets from an external store as files in the pod when it starts, and can optionally create a Secret too. Values need not be stored in etcd if you only use the mount. The pod depends on the store being reachable at start, so plan for outages and caching.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"encrypted-files-with-sops\">Encrypted files with SOPS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>SOPS encrypts the values in a file with a key from a cloud key service or an age key, so the file can be committed safely and decrypted by the deployment tool. It suits small teams and bootstrap secrets. Access control moves to who can use the key, and rotation needs a re encryption step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"environment-variables-or-files\">Environment variables or files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Environment variables are simple but appear in process listings, crash dumps and child processes, and do not change without a restart. Files can be updated in place and read on demand. Prefer files for long lived pods that must pick up rotated values, and verify that the application re reads them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"store&gt;sync;sync&gt;pod;pod&gt;rot\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Delivering secrets to Kubernetes workloads\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"store\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Secret store\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sync\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Operator or driver\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rot\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rotation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Secret store to Operator or driver; 2, Operator or driver to Pod; 3, Pod to Rotation.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"store\">\u003cdt>Secret store \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Source of truth.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unreachable.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sync\">\u003cdt>Operator or driver \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sync or mount.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reads file.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Old value.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rot\">\u003cdt>Rotation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> New version.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No reload.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-rotation-that-the-pods-never-saw\">Incident: The rotation that the pods never saw\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A database password was rotated and the application kept failing until every pod was restarted.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get externalsecret db-creds -n shop -o jsonpath=&quot;{.status.refreshTime}&quot;\nkubectl exec deploy/orders -n shop -- printenv DB_PASSWORD | wc -c\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get externalsecret db-creds -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{.status.refreshTime}&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-11-10T08:00:12Z\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> deploy/orders -n shop -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">printenv\u003c/span> DB_PASSWORD | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -c\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">17\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The operator refreshed the Secret on time, but the application read the password from an environment variable at start, so running pods kept the old value and failed after the old password was revoked. The team mounted the value as a file, made the application re read it on failure and added a rolling restart trigger when the Secret changed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Believing base64 means encrypted\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No encryption at rest for etcd\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Broad RBAC that lets everyone list Secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming running pods see rotated values\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Depending on the store with no plan for outage\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"native-secrets","text":"Native Secrets"},{"id":"external-secrets-operator","text":"External Secrets Operator"},{"id":"secrets-store-csi-driver","text":"Secrets Store CSI driver"},{"id":"encrypted-files-with-sops","text":"Encrypted files with SOPS"},{"id":"environment-variables-or-files","text":"Environment variables or files"},{"id":"incident-the-rotation-that-the-pods-never-saw","text":"Incident: The rotation that the pods never saw"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes Secrets","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/"},{"t":"Encrypting confidential data at rest","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/encrypt-data/"},{"t":"External Secrets Operator","u":"https://github.com/external-secrets/external-secrets"}],"text":"A Kubernetes Secret is encoded, not encrypted by default, and anyone with read access to it or to etcd can see it. The question is where the real secret lives and how it reaches the pod. What you will learn What a native Secret does and does not protect External Secrets Operator: sync from a store Secrets Store CSI driver: mount at start Encrypted files in a repository with SOPS Environment variables versus files and refresh Native Secrets A Secret is base64 encoded data stored in etcd. Enable encryption at rest for etcd, restrict who can get and list Secrets with RBAC, and avoid mounting them where not needed. Treat access to a namespace Secret as access to the secret, because pods in the namespace can read it. External Secrets Operator The operator reads values from an external store such as a cloud secret manager or Vault and creates native Secrets in the cluster. You describe the mapping in a custom resource, and the operator refreshes it on an interval. This keeps the source of truth outside the cluster and supports rotation, at the cost that the values still exist as Secrets inside. Secrets Store CSI driver The driver mounts secrets from an external store as files in the pod when it starts, and can optionally create a Secret too. Values need not be stored in etcd if you only use the mount. The pod depends on the store being reachable at start, so plan for outages and caching. Encrypted files with SOPS SOPS encrypts the values in a file with a key from a cloud key service or an age key, so the file can be committed safely and decrypted by the deployment tool. It suits small teams and bootstrap secrets. Access control moves to who can use the key, and rotation needs a re encryption step. Environment variables or files Environment variables are simple but appear in process listings, crash dumps and child processes, and do not change without a restart. Files can be updated in place and read on demand. Prefer files for long lived pods that must pick up rotated values, and verify that the application re reads them. Delivering secrets to Kubernetes workloads Flow in order: 1, Secret store to Operator or driver; 2, Operator or driver to Pod; 3, Pod to Rotation. Data store Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Secret store Data store What it does: Source of truth. If it fails or is weak: Unreachable. Operator or driver Step What it does: Sync or mount. If it fails or is weak: Stale. Pod Service What it does: Reads file. If it fails or is weak: Old value. Rotation Step What it does: New version. If it fails or is weak: No reload. Incident: The rotation that the pods never saw Incident A database password was rotated and the application kept failing until every pod was restarted. $ kubectl get externalsecret db-creds -n shop -o jsonpath= \"{.status.refreshTime}\" 2026-11-10T08:00:12Z $ kubectl exec deploy/orders -n shop -- printenv DB_PASSWORD | wc -c 17 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The operator refreshed the Secret on time, but the application read the password from an environment variable at start, so running pods kept the old value and failed after the old password was revoked. The team mounted the value as a file, made the application re read it on failure and added a rolling restart trigger when the Secret changed. Common mistakes Believing base64 means encrypted No encryption at rest for etcd Broad RBAC that lets everyone list Secrets Assuming running pods see rotated values Depending on the store with no plan for outage"},"secrets-pki/certificate-inventory-expiry-monitoring-and-revocation":{"html":"\u003cp>Expired certificates cause some of the most avoidable outages, because the date is known months ahead. The hard part is not the renewal command but knowing that the certificate exists.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Where certificates hide\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building an inventory from several sources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expiry alerts that reach a person who can act\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How revocation works: CRL, OCSP and stapling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Short lifetimes as the main defence\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-they-hide\">Where they hide\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Public sites, internal services, load balancers, mail servers, VPN gateways, databases, client certificates, code signing certificates and certificates embedded in appliances. Many are issued by different teams and renewed by hand once a year, which is exactly how they get forgotten.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inventory-sources\">Inventory sources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Combine several views: certificate transparency logs for public names, cloud certificate manager listings, Kubernetes certificate objects, scans of your address ranges for TLS endpoints and the issuance logs of your internal CA. Record the owner, the names, the issuer, the expiry and how it is renewed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"alerts\">Alerts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Probe the real endpoint and read the certificate that clients see, because the one in a store may not be the one deployed. Alert at thirty, fourteen and seven days, and route to the owning team. Add a check on the renewal process itself, such as a failed automatic renewal, since that appears weeks before the expiry.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"revocation\">Revocation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A certificate authority can revoke a certificate and publish that in a revocation list or answer status queries with OCSP. In practice many clients do not check, or fail open if the check fails. Stapling lets the server attach a fresh signed status. Because revocation is unreliable, design so that a stolen key becomes useless soon.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"short-lifetimes\">Short lifetimes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Certificates valid for days or weeks limit the damage from a stolen key and force automation to work. Public certificate lifetimes are being shortened across the industry, so manual renewal will not survive. Automate issuance and renewal with ACME or an internal CA, and treat a manual step as a defect.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"find&gt;inv;inv&gt;mon;mon&gt;auto\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Certificate inventory, expiry monitoring and revocation\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"find\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Discovery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"inv\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Inventory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mon\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Expiry monitor\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"auto\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Automated renewal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Discovery to Inventory; 2, Inventory to Expiry monitor; 3, Expiry monitor to Automated renewal.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"find\">\u003cdt>Discovery \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Logs and scans.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Gaps.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"inv\">\u003cdt>Inventory \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Owner and expiry.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mon\">\u003cdt>Expiry monitor \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Probe endpoints.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong source.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"auto\">\u003cdt>Automated renewal \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Short lifetime.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Manual.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-certificate-on-the-forgotten-gateway\">Incident: The certificate on the forgotten gateway\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A partner integration failed at night because a certificate on an old gateway expired.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"echo | openssl s_client -connect gw.example.com:443 -servername gw.example.com 2&gt;/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> | openssl s_client -connect gw.example.com:443 -servername gw.example.com 2&gt;/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">notAfter=Nov 10 23:59:59 2026 GMT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The gateway was set up three years earlier by a team that had since moved, and its certificate was renewed by hand each year. It was not in any inventory and no alert covered it. Engineers replaced the certificate that night. Afterwards they scanned all address ranges for TLS endpoints, built an inventory with owners and moved the gateway to automatic renewal with an expiry probe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Trusting a store listing instead of the live endpoint\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerting only a shared mailbox\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relying on revocation to contain a stolen key\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Manual renewal of any certificate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not covering client and code signing certificates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-they-hide","text":"Where they hide"},{"id":"inventory-sources","text":"Inventory sources"},{"id":"alerts","text":"Alerts"},{"id":"revocation","text":"Revocation"},{"id":"short-lifetimes","text":"Short lifetimes"},{"id":"incident-the-certificate-on-the-forgotten-gateway","text":"Incident: The certificate on the forgotten gateway"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Certificate transparency logs","u":"https://letsencrypt.org/docs/ct-logs/"},{"t":"Let's Encrypt: revocation and lifetimes","u":"https://letsencrypt.org/docs/"},{"t":"ACME protocol RFC 8555","u":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8555"}],"text":"Expired certificates cause some of the most avoidable outages, because the date is known months ahead. The hard part is not the renewal command but knowing that the certificate exists. What you will learn Where certificates hide Building an inventory from several sources Expiry alerts that reach a person who can act How revocation works: CRL, OCSP and stapling Short lifetimes as the main defence Where they hide Public sites, internal services, load balancers, mail servers, VPN gateways, databases, client certificates, code signing certificates and certificates embedded in appliances. Many are issued by different teams and renewed by hand once a year, which is exactly how they get forgotten. Inventory sources Combine several views: certificate transparency logs for public names, cloud certificate manager listings, Kubernetes certificate objects, scans of your address ranges for TLS endpoints and the issuance logs of your internal CA. Record the owner, the names, the issuer, the expiry and how it is renewed. Alerts Probe the real endpoint and read the certificate that clients see, because the one in a store may not be the one deployed. Alert at thirty, fourteen and seven days, and route to the owning team. Add a check on the renewal process itself, such as a failed automatic renewal, since that appears weeks before the expiry. Revocation A certificate authority can revoke a certificate and publish that in a revocation list or answer status queries with OCSP. In practice many clients do not check, or fail open if the check fails. Stapling lets the server attach a fresh signed status. Because revocation is unreliable, design so that a stolen key becomes useless soon. Short lifetimes Certificates valid for days or weeks limit the damage from a stolen key and force automation to work. Public certificate lifetimes are being shortened across the industry, so manual renewal will not survive. Automate issuance and renewal with ACME or an internal CA, and treat a manual step as a defect. Certificate inventory, expiry monitoring and revocation Flow in order: 1, Discovery to Inventory; 2, Inventory to Expiry monitor; 3, Expiry monitor to Automated renewal. Observability Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Discovery Observability What it does: Logs and scans. If it fails or is weak: Gaps. Inventory Data store What it does: Owner and expiry. If it fails or is weak: Stale. Expiry monitor Observability What it does: Probe endpoints. If it fails or is weak: Wrong source. Automated renewal Step What it does: Short lifetime. If it fails or is weak: Manual. Incident: The certificate on the forgotten gateway Incident A partner integration failed at night because a certificate on an old gateway expired. $ echo | openssl s_client -connect gw.example.com:443 -servername gw.example.com 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate notAfter=Nov 10 23:59:59 2026 GMT (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The gateway was set up three years earlier by a team that had since moved, and its certificate was renewed by hand each year. It was not in any inventory and no alert covered it. Engineers replaced the certificate that night. Afterwards they scanned all address ranges for TLS endpoints, built an inventory with owners and moved the gateway to automatic renewal with an expiry probe. Common mistakes Trusting a store listing instead of the live endpoint Alerting only a shared mailbox Relying on revocation to contain a stolen key Manual renewal of any certificate Not covering client and code signing certificates"},"secrets-pki/operating-cert-manager-issuers-acme-and-failures":{"html":"\u003cp>cert-manager turns certificates into Kubernetes objects that renew themselves. It works quietly until a challenge fails, a rate limit is hit or a webhook is down, so you need to know how to read its objects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The objects: Issuer, Certificate, CertificateRequest, Order and Challenge\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>HTTP-01 and DNS-01 and when to use each\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Renewal timing and secret handling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rate limits and staging environments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A debugging path from Certificate to Challenge\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-objects\">The objects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You define an Issuer or ClusterIssuer that describes the authority, and a Certificate that asks for names. cert-manager creates a CertificateRequest, then for ACME an Order and one Challenge per name, and finally stores the result in a Secret. Each object has status conditions, and the chain shows where it stopped.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"http-01-and-dns-01\">HTTP-01 and DNS-01\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>HTTP-01 proves control by serving a token over port 80, so it needs the name to be reachable from the internet and cannot issue wildcards. DNS-01 proves control by creating a TXT record, so it works for wildcards and private services, but the controller needs limited permission to edit the DNS zone. Scope that permission to the challenge records.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"renewal\">Renewal\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>By default cert-manager renews when about a third of the lifetime remains, and updates the Secret in place. Applications and ingress controllers must reload the new files, so test that they do. Set an alert for certificates that are not ready or are close to expiry, using the metrics that cert-manager exports.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rate-limits\">Rate limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Public authorities limit how many certificates can be requested for a name in a period. A retry loop in a broken setup can hit the limit and block you for days. Develop against the staging endpoint, avoid issuing many separate certificates for one domain and reuse a wildcard where it makes sense.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"debugging-path\">Debugging path\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run describe on the Certificate, then on its CertificateRequest, Order and Challenge, reading the events at each level. Common causes are an ingress that does not route the challenge path, a missing DNS permission, a webhook that is unavailable after an upgrade and a clock or network problem. Fix the cause and delete the failed Challenge to retry.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"cert&gt;ord;ord&gt;iss;iss&gt;sec\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Operating cert-manager: issuers, ACME and failures\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cert\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Certificate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ord\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Order and Challenge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"iss\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Issuer and CA\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sec\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Secret and ingress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Certificate to Order and Challenge; 2, Order and Challenge to Issuer and CA; 3, Issuer and CA to Secret and ingress.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cert\">\u003cdt>Certificate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Names wanted.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not ready.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ord\">\u003cdt>Order and Challenge \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Proof of control.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Pending.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"iss\">\u003cdt>Issuer and CA \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Signs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rate limit.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sec\">\u003cdt>Secret and ingress \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Serves it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No reload.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-certificate-that-stayed-pending-for-a-day\">Incident: A certificate that stayed pending for a day\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new service had no certificate after the deployment and the ingress served a default one.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe challenge -n shop | grep -E &quot;State|Reason&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe challenge -n shop | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;State|Reason&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">State:   pending\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Reason:  Waiting for HTTP-01 challenge propagation: wrong status code '404', expected '200'\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The ingress rule for the service forced a redirect and a rewrite that sent the challenge path to the application, which returned 404. The team allowed the challenge path through, deleted the failed Challenge so that it retried, and the certificate became ready in a minute. They added a test that requests a certificate in staging for every new ingress pattern.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the production endpoint while testing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting DNS edit rights to the whole zone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not reloading applications after renewal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the Challenge events\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Issuing many certificates for one domain\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-objects","text":"The objects"},{"id":"http-01-and-dns-01","text":"HTTP-01 and DNS-01"},{"id":"renewal","text":"Renewal"},{"id":"rate-limits","text":"Rate limits"},{"id":"debugging-path","text":"Debugging path"},{"id":"incident-a-certificate-that-stayed-pending-for-a-day","text":"Incident: A certificate that stayed pending for a day"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"cert-manager","u":"https://github.com/cert-manager/cert-manager"},{"t":"ACME protocol RFC 8555","u":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8555"},{"t":"Let's Encrypt: revocation and lifetimes","u":"https://letsencrypt.org/docs/"}],"text":"cert-manager turns certificates into Kubernetes objects that renew themselves. It works quietly until a challenge fails, a rate limit is hit or a webhook is down, so you need to know how to read its objects. What you will learn The objects: Issuer, Certificate, CertificateRequest, Order and Challenge HTTP-01 and DNS-01 and when to use each Renewal timing and secret handling Rate limits and staging environments A debugging path from Certificate to Challenge The objects You define an Issuer or ClusterIssuer that describes the authority, and a Certificate that asks for names. cert-manager creates a CertificateRequest, then for ACME an Order and one Challenge per name, and finally stores the result in a Secret. Each object has status conditions, and the chain shows where it stopped. HTTP-01 and DNS-01 HTTP-01 proves control by serving a token over port 80, so it needs the name to be reachable from the internet and cannot issue wildcards. DNS-01 proves control by creating a TXT record, so it works for wildcards and private services, but the controller needs limited permission to edit the DNS zone. Scope that permission to the challenge records. Renewal By default cert-manager renews when about a third of the lifetime remains, and updates the Secret in place. Applications and ingress controllers must reload the new files, so test that they do. Set an alert for certificates that are not ready or are close to expiry, using the metrics that cert-manager exports. Rate limits Public authorities limit how many certificates can be requested for a name in a period. A retry loop in a broken setup can hit the limit and block you for days. Develop against the staging endpoint, avoid issuing many separate certificates for one domain and reuse a wildcard where it makes sense. Debugging path Run describe on the Certificate, then on its CertificateRequest, Order and Challenge, reading the events at each level. Common causes are an ingress that does not route the challenge path, a missing DNS permission, a webhook that is unavailable after an upgrade and a clock or network problem. Fix the cause and delete the failed Challenge to retry. Operating cert-manager: issuers, ACME and failures Flow in order: 1, Certificate to Order and Challenge; 2, Order and Challenge to Issuer and CA; 3, Issuer and CA to Secret and ingress. Service Step Outside party Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Certificate Service What it does: Names wanted. If it fails or is weak: Not ready. Order and Challenge Step What it does: Proof of control. If it fails or is weak: Pending. Issuer and CA Outside party What it does: Signs. If it fails or is weak: Rate limit. Secret and ingress Service What it does: Serves it. If it fails or is weak: No reload. Incident: A certificate that stayed pending for a day Incident A new service had no certificate after the deployment and the ingress served a default one. $ kubectl describe challenge -n shop | grep -E \"State|Reason\" State: pending Reason: Waiting for HTTP-01 challenge propagation: wrong status code '404', expected '200' (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The ingress rule for the service forced a redirect and a rewrite that sent the challenge path to the application, which returned 404. The team allowed the challenge path through, deleted the failed Challenge so that it retried, and the certificate became ready in a minute. They added a test that requests a certificate in staging for every new ingress pattern. Common mistakes Using the production endpoint while testing Granting DNS edit rights to the whole zone Not reloading applications after renewal Ignoring the Challenge events Issuing many certificates for one domain"},"secrets-pki/workload-identity-spiffe-and-key-management":{"html":"\u003cp>The safest secret is the one that never exists. If a workload can prove who it is, a system can give it a short lived credential without any password being stored.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Identity instead of stored secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>OIDC federation for CI and Kubernetes service accounts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>SPIFFE and SPIRE for service identity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Key management services and envelope encryption\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Key rotation, access policy and audit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"identity-instead-of-secrets\">Identity instead of secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A workload presents a signed token that says who it is and where it runs, and the receiving system maps that to permissions. No long lived password is created, copied or leaked. This is the direction of most modern platforms, and it also makes audits clearer, because access is tied to a named workload.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"oidc-federation\">OIDC federation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A pipeline or a pod receives a short lived signed token from its platform. A cloud role is configured to trust that issuer and to accept only tokens with a specific subject, such as one repository and branch. The workload exchanges the token for temporary cloud credentials. Restrict the conditions tightly, because a broad trust lets other workloads assume the role.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"spiffe-and-spire\">SPIFFE and SPIRE\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>SPIFFE defines a standard identity name for a workload and a format for proving it, usually an X.509 certificate or a signed token. SPIRE is an implementation that attests workloads using properties of the node and the pod and then issues short lived identities. Services use them for mutual TLS without managing certificates by hand.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"key-management\">Key management\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A key management service stores master keys in hardware backed storage and never reveals them. Envelope encryption uses a data key to encrypt data and the master key to encrypt the data key, so large data stays local and only small requests go to the service. Policies decide which identities can use each key.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rotation-and-audit\">Rotation and audit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Rotate master keys on a schedule, which usually keeps old versions for decrypting existing data. Limit key administration and key use to different roles, and send every key use to an audit log. Alert on unusual decrypt volume, since mass decryption is a sign of theft.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"wl&gt;idp;idp&gt;role;role&gt;kms\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Workload identity, SPIFFE and key management\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Workload\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"idp\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Token issuer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"role\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Role or policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kms\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Key service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Workload to Token issuer; 2, Token issuer to Role or policy; 3, Role or policy to Key service.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wl\">\u003cdt>Workload \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Gets a token.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Broad trust.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"idp\">\u003cdt>Token issuer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Signs identity.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong subject.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"role\">\u003cdt>Role or policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Temporary access.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Over broad.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kms\">\u003cdt>Key service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Envelope keys.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unaudited.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-role-that-any-repository-could-assume\">Incident: A role that any repository could assume\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A federation trust accepted tokens from any repository in the organisation.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam get-role --role-name deploy-prod --query &quot;Role.AssumeRolePolicyDocument.Statement[0].Condition&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam get-role --role-name deploy-prod --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Role.AssumeRolePolicyDocument.Statement[0].Condition&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;StringLike&quot;: {&quot;token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub&quot;: &quot;repo:example-org/*&quot;}}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The role trusted every repository in the organisation, so a test repository with weak review could obtain production deploy rights. A review found it before misuse. The team limited the condition to one repository and one protected environment, added a policy test that rejects wildcards in the subject, and logged every assumption of the role.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Wildcard subjects in a federation trust\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping long lived keys beside the federation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing data keys unencrypted\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Same role for key admin and key use\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on unusual decrypt activity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"identity-instead-of-secrets","text":"Identity instead of secrets"},{"id":"oidc-federation","text":"OIDC federation"},{"id":"spiffe-and-spire","text":"SPIFFE and SPIRE"},{"id":"key-management","text":"Key management"},{"id":"rotation-and-audit","text":"Rotation and audit"},{"id":"incident-a-role-that-any-repository-could-assume","text":"Incident: A role that any repository could assume"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SPIFFE and SPIRE","u":"https://github.com/spiffe/spire"},{"t":"Kubernetes service account tokens","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/service-accounts/"},{"t":"AWS KMS concepts","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html"}],"text":"The safest secret is the one that never exists. If a workload can prove who it is, a system can give it a short lived credential without any password being stored. What you will learn Identity instead of stored secrets OIDC federation for CI and Kubernetes service accounts SPIFFE and SPIRE for service identity Key management services and envelope encryption Key rotation, access policy and audit Identity instead of secrets A workload presents a signed token that says who it is and where it runs, and the receiving system maps that to permissions. No long lived password is created, copied or leaked. This is the direction of most modern platforms, and it also makes audits clearer, because access is tied to a named workload. OIDC federation A pipeline or a pod receives a short lived signed token from its platform. A cloud role is configured to trust that issuer and to accept only tokens with a specific subject, such as one repository and branch. The workload exchanges the token for temporary cloud credentials. Restrict the conditions tightly, because a broad trust lets other workloads assume the role. SPIFFE and SPIRE SPIFFE defines a standard identity name for a workload and a format for proving it, usually an X.509 certificate or a signed token. SPIRE is an implementation that attests workloads using properties of the node and the pod and then issues short lived identities. Services use them for mutual TLS without managing certificates by hand. Key management A key management service stores master keys in hardware backed storage and never reveals them. Envelope encryption uses a data key to encrypt data and the master key to encrypt the data key, so large data stays local and only small requests go to the service. Policies decide which identities can use each key. Rotation and audit Rotate master keys on a schedule, which usually keeps old versions for decrypting existing data. Limit key administration and key use to different roles, and send every key use to an audit log. Alert on unusual decrypt volume, since mass decryption is a sign of theft. Workload identity, SPIFFE and key management Flow in order: 1, Workload to Token issuer; 2, Token issuer to Role or policy; 3, Role or policy to Key service. Service Security Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Workload Service What it does: Gets a token. If it fails or is weak: Broad trust. Token issuer Security What it does: Signs identity. If it fails or is weak: Wrong subject. Role or policy Security What it does: Temporary access. If it fails or is weak: Over broad. Key service Data store What it does: Envelope keys. If it fails or is weak: Unaudited. Incident: A role that any repository could assume Incident A federation trust accepted tokens from any repository in the organisation. $ aws iam get-role --role-name deploy-prod --query \"Role.AssumeRolePolicyDocument.Statement[0].Condition\" {\"StringLike\": {\"token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub\": \"repo:example-org/*\"}} (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The role trusted every repository in the organisation, so a test repository with weak review could obtain production deploy rights. A review found it before misuse. The team limited the condition to one repository and one protected environment, added a policy test that rejects wildcards in the subject, and logged every assumption of the role. Common mistakes Wildcard subjects in a federation trust Keeping long lived keys beside the federation Storing data keys unencrypted Same role for key admin and key use No alert on unusual decrypt activity"}});